  "final_text": "**No, not yet. Fix CRAFT first, then revisit the marketing spend.** I recalculated the figures from the raw CSVs, and both of the VP's arguments come from how the numbers are counted, not from real change.\n\n## The two claims, checked\n\n**1. \"Units up 22.4% on August\"**\n- **What's counted:** CRAFT units in `demand_actuals.csv`, summed by month.\n- **The problem:** August has 4 weekly buckets and September has 5 (week starts 08-05 to 08-26, then 09-02 to 09-30). The report's §6 already warns about this.\n\n| | Aug (4 wks) | Sep (5 wks) | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Monthly total | 7,488 | 9,162 | **+22.4%** (as reported) |\n| Per week | 1,872 | 1,832 | **−2.1%** |\n\nI recalculated: September's 9,162 units comes from `demand_actuals.csv`, line = CRAFT, week_start in 2023‑09, 5 weeks (1,874 + 1,624 + 1,877 + 1,752 + 2,035). It matches the report exactly, and so does the €4,124,238 revenue. **Per week, CRAFT demand went down slightly.** All of the growth is the extra week. That last week also starts 09-30 and is labelled FY24‑Q3‑W01, so \"September\" includes a week of Q3.\n\n**2. \"Defects fell from 3.21 to 2.51 per 1,000 units\"**\n- **What's counted:** the number of defect records (NCRs) per 1,000 units sold. That's 24 / 7,488 in August and 23 / 9,162 in September.\n- **The problem:** the number of units sold grew only because September had an extra week, so the rate falls without anything improving. Counting records also ignores how big each defect batch was.\n\nHere is the same data counted by **units affected** instead of number of records (`ncr_records.csv`, `qty_affected`):\n\n| CRAFT | Aug | Sep |\n|---|---|---|\n| Defect records (NCRs) | 24 | 23 |\n| Units affected | 2,324 | 2,832 |\n| Units affected ÷ units sold | **31.0%** | **30.9%** |\n| Scrapped units | 924 | 1,539 |\n| Scrapped units per week | 231 | **308 (+33%)** |\n\nCounted in units, CRAFT quality is **flat at about 31%**, and **scrap is getting worse**. One caveat on that ratio: it divides production defects by sales units, so read it as an indicator, not a true defect rate. Even so, CRAFT has the highest record count of any line in both months: 23 vs 19 for NEST, the next highest.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n- **Decision:** Don't move Q3 marketing to CRAFT now. Rework the line first.\n- **Why:**\n  - Per week, demand is −2.1%, not +22.4%.\n  - Counted in units, quality hasn't improved (31.0% → 30.9%), and scrap is up 33% per week.\n  - More demand means more CRAFT production, and roughly a third of the volume is currently scrapped or reworked.\n- **Unknowns that add risk:**\n  - **Margin:** \"Revenue\" is units × list price, and there's no cost data, so CRAFT's €450 average price doesn't show it's profitable (§6).\n  - **Marketing payoff:** the €2.03M in budgets can't be tied to a product line, so you couldn't measure whether extra CRAFT spend worked (§4, §6).\n- **Risks either way:**\n  - If you hold back, CRAFT may lose some Q3 sales on a high-priced line.\n  - If you push now, you increase scrap and rework, and possibly returns. There's no data on returns per line, so you'd be exposed without being able to see it.\n- **The VP's case at its strongest:** counting defect records per 1,000 units, CRAFT did improve (3.21 → 2.51), and it's the only line that did. Records per week also fell, from 6.0 to 4.6. But that measure can improve just by logging fewer, larger defect records. The number of units affected didn't fall.\n- **What would change this:** review again when, over at least 4 comparable weeks:\n  - CRAFT scrapped plus reworked units per week stay clearly below August's 581 (my suggestion: under about 290, i.e. half), and\n  - scrapped units per week fall below August's 231, and\n  - per-week sales hold at or above about 1,870.\n\n  I set these thresholds in units on purpose, because a record count can be lowered by grouping defects without fixing anything.\n\nThere was no team file of metric definitions to check against, so the metric definitions above are my own, based on the report's §6 notes.",
